{{also|Alcaide}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|alcayde}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|es|alcaide}}, from {{der|en|ar|قائد|الْقَائِد|leader}}; compare {{m|en|caid}}. {{root|en|ar|ق و د}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ælˈkaɪdi/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[governor]] or [[commander]] of a [[Spanish]] or [[Portuguese]] [[fortress]] or prison.
#* '''1768''', Edward Cavendish Drake, ''A New Universal Collection of Authentic Voyages and Travels'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=Es-a4ISg0UMC&pg=PA510&dq=%22alcaide%22%7C%22alcaides%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwj3pfTTvqzQAhUJx7wKHVIZDOEQ6AEIdDAQ#v=onepage&q=%22alcaide%22%7C%22alcaides%22&f=false page 510],
#*: Soon after Mr. Petticrew arrived at Gibraltar, he came to {{w|Tétouan|Tetuan}} in his majeſty's ſhip Seahorſe, to acquaint the '''alcaide''' that he had orders from the king of Great Britain,{{...}}.
#* '''1810''', John Joseph Stockdale (editor and publisher), ''The History of the Inquisitions'', extract published in 1810, ''The Literary Panorama, and National Register'', Volume 8, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ZWU3AQAAMAAJ&pg=PT103&dq=%22It+was,+above+all,+to+the+alcaide+and+the+guards+of+the+prisoners+that+he+studied+to+recommend+himself%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwj1qqvOpq3QAhWBvLwKHTU6A1kQ6AEIHDAA#v=onepage&q=%22It%20was%2C%20above%20all%2C%20to%20the%20alcaide%20and%20the%20guards%20of%20the%20prisoners%20that%20he%20studied%20to%20recommend%20himself%22&f=false page 219],
#*: It was, above all, to the '''alcaide''' and the guards of the prisoners that he studied to recommend himself.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1825|title=The Literary Chronicle for the year 1825|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=7nQPAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA172&dq=%22alcaide%22%7C%22alcaides%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwj3pfTTvqzQAhUJx7wKHVIZDOEQ6AEIjgIwKg#v=onepage&q=%22alcaide%22%7C%22alcaides%22&f=false|page=172
|passage=The municipal bodies were charged regularly to inspect the prisons; to watch over the conduct of the '''alcaides''', and the inferior officers; and to propose to the government such measures as they judged to be best conducive to humanity and sound policy.}}
